===National Coalition Party===

The incumbent president [[Sauli Niinistö]] was elected as the candidate of the [[National Coalition Party]] in the 2012 election. He was eligible for re-election and his decision for running again was closely followed throughout the latter half of his first term.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/presidentti_niinisto_jatkohalut_julki_noin_vuosi_ennen_vaaleja/7836454 |title=Presidentti Niinistö: Jatkohalut julki noin vuosi ennen vaaleja |website=Yle |date=28 February 2015 |access-date=17 March 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> On 29 May 2017, Niinistö announced that he would seek support for his candidacy as an independent candidate outside party politics. To become an official candidate, Niinistö needed 20,000 signatures from his supporters.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9637194 |title=Sauli Niinistö hakee jatkokautta presidenttinä – mittauttaa suosionsa yhdistyksen avulla |website=Yle |date=29 May 2017 |access-date=29 May 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> Niinistö eventually gathered 156,000 signatures and his candidacy was confirmed on 25 September.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.niinisto.fi/2017/09/25/sauli-niinistolle-156-000-kannattajakorttia/|title=Sauli Niinistölle 156 000 kannattajakorttia|trans-title=Sauli Niinistö gathers 156 000 signatures|date=25 September 2017|access-date=25 September 2017|website=Sauli Niinistö|language=fi|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170925180847/https://www.niinisto.fi/2017/09/25/sauli-niinistolle-156-000-kannattajakorttia/|archive-date=25 September 2017|url-status = dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref>

Soon after Niinistö's announcement, the leader of the National Coalition Party [[Petteri Orpo]] [[Twitter|tweeted]] that Niinistö had the party's full support.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9637391 |title=Petteri Orpo twiittasi heti Niinistön jatkokausi-ilmoituksen jälkeen: "Kokoomus on täysillä mukana" |website=Yle |date=29 May 2017 |access-date=29 May 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

===Centre Party===
[[File:Matti Vanhanen 2017 06.jpg|thumb|Former prime minister [[Matti Vanhanen]] was the Centre Party's candidate]]

The [[Centre Party (Finland)|Centre Party]] decided on 30 November 2015 that the party would choose their presidential candidate already in June 2016.<ref name="Suomenmaa">{{cite web|url=http://www.suomenmaa.fi/?app=NeoDirect&com=6/3/92303/f27553d053&t=Keskustalta-yllatysveto-Presidenttiehdokas-jo-kesalla--Sipila-kieltaytyy#4403 |title=Keskustalta yllätysveto: Presidenttiehdokas jo kesällä - Sipilä kieltäytyy |website=Suomenmaa |date=15 March 2016 |access-date=14 April 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Soon after, former prime minister and Centre Party's presidential candidate in 2006 election, [[Matti Vanhanen]], announced that he would run for candidacy.<ref name="HS173">{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/a1458188578788?ref=hs-prio-4-3 |title=Vanhanen lähtee presidenttikisaan ja luopuu keskustan eduskuntaryhmän johdosta |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=17 March 2016 |access-date=17 March 2016 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>[https://sites.google.com/site/vanhanenpresidentiksi/ Matti Vanhanen presidentiksi] (in Finnish). Retrieved 2016-05-21.</ref> Prime Minister [[Juha Sipilä]] declined his interest early on.<ref name="Suomenmaa"/> Other prominent names in speculations for the candidacy were the former prime ministers [[Esko Aho]] and [[Anneli Jäätteenmäki]], and former minister of Economic Affairs [[Olli Rehn]].<ref name="Karjalainen">{{cite web |url=http://www.karjalainen.fi/mielipiteet/mielipiteet/kolumnit/item/41381-eikohan-jo-avata-presidenttipelikin |title=Eiköhän jo avata presidenttipelikin |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=4 January 2014 |access-date=17 March 2016 |language=fi |archive-date=11 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141111011250/http://www.karjalainen.fi/mielipiteet/mielipiteet/kolumnit/item/41381-eikohan-jo-avata-presidenttipelikin |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Iltalehti">{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/uutiset/a/2016022421164137 |title= Demarien presidenttipeli pyörii: Heinäluoma vai Urpilainen |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=23 February 2015 |access-date=17 March 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Rehn declined the possibility stating that it wouldn't be possible to combine his duties as cabinet minister with campaigning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000001171463.html |title= Olli Rehn ei lähde presidenttikisaan |website=Ilta-sanomat |date=3 May 2016 |access-date=3 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Aho did not comment his interest in candidacy, but it was considered unlikely for him to seek presidential nomination, as he was a candidate for the supervisory board of [[Sberbank of Russia|Sberbank]] at the time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000001140270.html |title= Ex-pääministeri Esko Aho ehdolla huippupaikalle venäläispankkiin |website=Ilta-sanomat |date=17 March 2016 |access-date=3 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Jäätteenmäki, an incumbent [[Vice President of the European Parliament|vice president of the European Parliament]], said running for president was "not on her agenda".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000001141136.html |title=Anneli Jäätteenmäki Suomen vaikeasta taloustilanteesta: "On aina niin helppo säästää toisten menoista" |website=Ilta-sanomat |date=20 March 2016 |access-date=6 June 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

As no challengers appeared till the deadline of 11 May, Vanhanen was the sole candidate in June's party congress and was confirmed as the Centre Party's candidate in the presidential election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/kotimaa/a1462936635076 |title=Matti Vanhasesta keskustan presidenttiehdokas – kukaan ei ilmoittautunut haastajaksi |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=11 May 2016 |access-date=11 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/uutiset/a/2016061221714870 |title=Matti Vanhasesta yksimielisesti keskustan presidenttiehdokas |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=12 June 2016 |access-date=12 June 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Vanhanen said that his candidacy was motivated by the support he felt he had around the country during his last campaign and the will to improve the security situation in the areas surrounding Finland.<ref>[https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000001140341.html Matti Vanhanen kertoo nyt, miksi hän haluaa Suomen presidentiksi] (in Finnish). ''Ilta Sanomat''. Retrieved 2016-05-21.</ref>

===Finns Party===
[[File:Laura Huhtasaari crop.jpg|thumb|Finns Party MP [[Laura Huhtasaari]] was nominated as the party's candidate]]

The Finns Party was expected to confirm their candidate in the summer of 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.verkkouutiset.fi/kotimaa/sampo%20terho-52121 |title=Hänestäkö PS:n presidenttiehdokas? "En missään nimessä sulje pois" |website=Verkkouutiset |date=24 June 2016 |access-date=24 June 2016 |language=fi }}{{Dead link|date=September 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> The leader of the [[Finns Party]] [[Timo Soini]] announced early on in November 2014 that he would not seek candidacy in the 2018 presidential election, after getting 3,43 % and 9,4 % of votes in [[2006 Finnish presidential election|2006]] and [[2012 Finnish presidential election|2012]] presidential elections respectively.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.apu.fi/artikkeli/timo-soini-en-lahde-enaa-presidenttiehdokkaaksi |title=Timo Soini: En lähde enää presidenttiehdokkaaksi |website=Apu |date=28 November 2014 |access-date=14 April 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> He reaffirmed his decision in April 2016, encouraging party to move on and inviting new faces to enter party's primaries.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000001158360.html |title=Timo Soini ei pyri presidentiksi – "Puolueen on aika siirtyä eteenpäin" |website=Ilta-sanomat |date=14 April 2016 |access-date=14 April 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> As Soini had been a strong face for the Finns Party, his decision sparked much speculation on the party's decision, as party's presidential candidate was expected to also follow Soini as the chairman.<ref name="HS210">{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/a1475375399412 |title=Miksi eturivin perussuomalaiset ilmoittavat kilvan harkitsevansa presidenttiehdokkuutta? Kyseessä on taistelu Timo Soinin perinnöstä ja seuraajasta |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=2 October 2016 |access-date=2 October 2016 |language=fi}}</ref>

In March 2017 Soini announced that he would not seek another term as the leader of the party. Soon after, the chairman of the parliamentary group [[Sampo Terho]] announced that he would seek chairmanship and, if elected, also presidential candidacy.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005115728.html |title=Perussuomalaisen puheenjohtajaksi pyrkivä Sampo Terho väläytteli Suomeen EU-kansanäänestystä – "Se voi olla kynnyskysymys" |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=6 March 2017 |access-date= 6 March 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> Member of the European Parliament [[Jussi Halla-aho]], Minister of Defence [[Jussi Niinistö]] and Speaker of the Parliament [[Maria Lohela]] did also express their interest in candidacy, while Minister of Justice and Labor [[Jari Lindström]] declined early on.<ref name="HS210"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/uutiset/a/201612132200040247 |title=Halla-aho valmis harkitsemaan presidenttiehdokkuutta - "en lähde sen kanssa sooloilemaan" |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=13 December 2016 |access-date=14 December 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> However, only Halla-aho decided to also seek chairmanship in the leadership election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Halla-aho's bid for Finns Party leadership boosts interest in party membership|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/osasto/news/halla-ahos_bid_for_finns_party_leadership_boosts_interest_in_party_membership/9543180|access-date=17 June 2017|website=Yle|date=2 April 2017}}</ref>

Choosing the presidential candidate for the party was on the agenda for party congress in June 2017. However, after Jussi Halla-aho won the [[2017 Finns Party leadership election|leadership election]], the decision was postponed by Halla-aho's request.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aamulehti.fi/kotimaa/perussuomalaiset-lykkasi-presidenttiehdokkaan-valintaa-varikkaiden-vaiheiden-jalkeen-200200611/ |title=Perussuomalaiset lykkäsi presidenttiehdokkaan valintaa värikkäiden vaiheiden jälkeen |website=Aamulehti |date=11 June 2017 |access-date=17 June 2017 |language=fi |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731064111/https://www.aamulehti.fi/kotimaa/perussuomalaiset-lykkasi-presidenttiehdokkaan-valintaa-varikkaiden-vaiheiden-jalkeen-200200611/ |archive-date=31 July 2017 |url-status=dead }}</ref> A few days after the leadership election, twenty Finns Party MPs, including all cabinet ministers, defected to form a new parliamentary group under the name [[New Alternative]].<ref>[https://yle.fi/uutiset/osasto/news/finns_party_splinters_new_group_makes_bid_for_sipila_government/9666946 Finns Party splinters, new group makes bid for Sipilä government] YLE 13.6.2017</ref> After the split, most of the potential presidential candidates had left the party. However, the newly elected vice-chairman [[Laura Huhtasaari]] and MP [[Tom Packalén]] announced that they were thinking about the candidacy.<ref name="Demokraatti">{{cite web|url=https://demokraatti.fi/kaksi-perussuomalaista-harkitsee-presidenttiehdokkuutta/ |title=Kaksi perussuomalaista harkitsee presidenttiehdokkuutta |website=Demokraatti |date=22 June 2017 |access-date=22 June 2017 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/politiikka/a/201707212200277944 |title=PS:n Tom Packalén harkitsee vakavasti presidenttiehdokkuutta: "Poliisikokemukseni myötä tunnen ihmisten erilaiset elämätilanteet" |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=21 July 2017 |access-date=21 July 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

On 4 August 2017 Halla-aho announced that the board of the Finns Party had chosen Huhtasaari as the presidential candidate of the party,<ref name="hshuhtasaari">{{cite news|title=Perussuomalaiset aikovat asettaa kansanedustaja Laura Huhtasaaren presidenttiehdokkaaksi|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005314879.html|access-date=4 August 2017|work=Helsingin Sanomat}}</ref> and her candidacy was confirmed by the party council on 23 September.

===Blue Reform===
On 19 June 2017 [[Sampo Terho]] announced that a new party would be formed based on the [[New Alternative]] parliamentary group under the name [[Blue Reform]].<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/osasto/news/finns_party_breakaway_group_to_be_named_blue_reform/9678859 | title=Finns Party breakaway group to be named Blue Reform | date=19 June 2017 | access-date=20 June 2017 | website = Yle}}</ref> The vice-chair of the Blue Reform parliamentary group [[Tiina Elovaara]] stated initially that the group was likely to have their own presidential candidate.<ref name="Demokraatti"/> However, as the party was formed after the previous parliamentary election and thus had no elected MPs, it would have required to gather a sufficient number of signatures to set an own candidate.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/politiikka/a/201707162200267837 | title=Uuden vaihtoehdon lähtö presidentinvaaleihin on vielä auki | date=16 July 2017 | access-date=17 July 2017 | work = Iltalehti}}</ref> Thus, ultimately, the party decided not to put forth their own candidate and neither did it formally back any running candidate.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.is.fi/presidentinvaalit2018/art-2000005417834.html | title=Siniset jättää presidentinvaalit väliin – ei asetu minkään ehdokkaan taakse | date=21 October 2017 | access-date=21 October 2017 | work = Ilta-Sanomat}}</ref>

===Green League===
[[File:Pekka Haavisto 2017 03.jpg|thumb|The 2012 presidential candidate for Green League, [[Pekka Haavisto]], reprised his candidacy]]

The party's 2012 presidential candidate, [[Pekka Haavisto]], announced in February 2017 that he will reprise his candidacy.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005084134.html |title=Vihreiden Pekka Haavisto lähtee presidenttikisaan – "Pitää katsoa eteenpäin seuraavaan sataan vuoteen" |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=11 February 2017 |access-date=11 February 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> The decision came after Haavisto had been approached multiple times by the Green Party.<ref name="HS15">{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/kotimaa/a1461984476887 |title=Vuoden 2018 presidenttipeli vielä auki – he saattavat havitella paikkaa |website=Helsingin sanomat |date=1 May 2016 |access-date=1 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Previously the party leader [[Ville Niinistö]], President Niinistö's nephew, had stated that he would not seek the candidacy.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/vihreille_presidenttiehdokas_viela_tana_vuonna/8898541?origin=rss |title=Vihreille presidenttiehdokas vielä tänä vuonna |website=Yle |date=21 May 2016 |access-date=21 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Haavisto was confirmed as the party's candidate on 12 February.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005084944.html |title=Pekka Haavisto kukitettiin vihreiden presidenttiehdokkaaksi - kilpailijoita ei ilmaantunut |work= Helsingin Sanomat |date=12 February 2017 |access-date=13 February 2017}}</ref>

===Left Alliance===
The Left Alliance chose MEP [[Merja Kyllönen]] as the party's candidate on 18 March 2017, after being the only one interested in running.<ref name="HS1002">{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005082533.html |title=Europarlamentaarikko Merja Kyllönen suostuu vasemmistoliiton presidenttiehdokkaaksi |website=Helsingin Sanomat|date=10 February 2017 |access-date=10 February 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> Former leader of the party [[Paavo Arhinmäki]] was also interested in running early on, but later decided to concentrate on running for the office of Mayor of Helsinki.<ref name="HS1002"/>

===Social Democratic Party===
Social Democratic Party organised an informal membership poll in August 2017 for electing the party's presidential candidate, with three candidates entering the race, MPs [[Maarit Feldt-Ranta]], [[Tuula Haatainen]] and [[Sirpa Paatero]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9696287?origin=rss |title=SDP vahvisti jäsenäänestykseen osallistuvat presidenttiehdokkaansa – yllätysnimiä ei tullut |work=Yle |date=29 June 2017 |access-date=29 June 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> The final decision based on the membership poll was made on 2 September 2017, when it was revealed that Haatainen had received the most votes in the poll. Haatainen ultimately gained 48.6% of the votes against Feldt-Ranta's 42.3% and Paatero's 8.6%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000005350892.html |title=Tuula Haatainen Sdp:n presidenttiehdokkaaksi |website=Ilta-Sanomat |date=2 September 2017 |access-date=2 September 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

Before Feldt-Ranta, Haatainen and Paatero entered the party primary, Social Democratic Party was struggling to find potential candidates, as most of the prominent politicians had declined the candidacy. From early on, there was speculation on two possible candidates, [[Eero Heinäluoma]] and [[Jutta Urpilainen]].<ref name="Iltalehti"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000000592198.html |title=Demarin ex-vetäjä: Heinäluoma tähtää presidentiksi |website=Ilta-Sanomat |date=8 March 2013 |access-date=17 March 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> In June 2016, Heinäluoma announced that he would not seek presidency due to his wife's recent death and ongoing work in the Parliament.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/sdpn_eero_heinaluoma_ei_pyri_presidentiksi__ehdottaa_urpilaista/8972191 |title=SDP:n Eero Heinäluoma ei pyri presidentiksi – ehdottaa Urpilaista |website=Yle |date=21 June 2016 |access-date=21 June 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> In February 2017, Urpilainen also announced she would not seek presidency.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000005099448.html |title=Demokraatti: Jutta Urpilainen ei lähde Sdp:n presidenttiehdokkaaksi |website=Ilta-Sanomat |date=23 February 2017 |access-date=23 February 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> The leader of the party [[Antti Rinne]], the governor of the Bank of Finland [[Erkki Liikanen]] and MEP [[Liisa Jaakonsaari]] likewise announced that they were not entering the presidential race.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/uutiset/a/201604150095818 |title=Antti Rinne ei lähde presidenttiehdokkaaksi |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=15 April 2016 |access-date=15 April 2016 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000000233632.html |title=Erkki Liikanen haluaa jatkaa Suomen Pankissa |website=Iltalehti |date=7 March 2009 |access-date=21 June 2016 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/uutiset/a/201703042200080570 |title=Liisa Jaakonsaari: "Kiva kun pyydetään, mutta en ole lähdössä presidenttiehdokkaaksi" |website=Iltalehti|url-access=registration|date=4 March 2017 |access-date=4 March 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> There were also talks within SDP on supporting a candidate outside the party, such as archbishop [[Kari Mäkinen]], if no candidate would be found from within.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/a1473299985184 |title=Sdp:ssä pohditaan kuumeisesti presidenttiehdokasta –  vaihtoehtoina ovat myös Sauli Niinistö ja arkkipiispa Kari Mäkinen |work=Helsingin sanomat |date=8 September 2016 |access-date=8 September 2016 |language=fi}}</ref> Prominent SDP figureheads, such as [[Erkki Tuomioja]] and [[Lasse Lehtinen]], even suggested the possibility of backing the incumbent president Sauli Niinistö.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9649779 |title=Erkki Tuomioja: SDP:n vakavasti harkittava, voisiko puolue tukea Sauli Niinistöä |website=Yle |date=5 June 2017 |access-date=20 June 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

===Swedish People's Party===
The [[Swedish People's Party of Finland|Swedish People's Party]] decided to choose their candidate in the party congress in June 2017. As no one else entered the party's primary on 11 June, Member of the European Parliament [[Nils Torvalds]] was nominated as the party candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9662767|title=RKP:n presidenttiehdokkaaksi valittiin europarlamentaarikko Nils Torvalds|date=13 June 2017|website=Yle|language=fi|access-date=13 June 2017}}</ref> In Spring 2016, then leader of the party, [[Carl Haglund]] stated that he was thinking about candidacy, but renounced his leadership and left politics later that year.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.suomenmaa.fi/?app=NeoDirect&com=6/3/115375/2e37ad773c |title=Carl Haglund presidenttiehdokuudestaan Ykkösaamussa: "Lievä ehkä" |website=Suomenmaa |date=21 May 2016 |access-date=21 May 2016 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ksml.fi/kotimaa/Haglund-j%C3%A4tt%C3%A4%C3%A4-politiikan-%E2%80%93-siirtyy-kiinalaisyritys-Kaidille/788235 |title=Haglund jättää politiikan – siirtyy kiinalaisyritys Kaidille |website=Keskisuomalainen |date=21 June 2016 |access-date=8 September 2016 |language=fi}}</ref>

===Christian Democrats===
On 19 August 2017, the [[Christian Democrats (Finland)|Christian Democrats]] decided to back the incumbent president Sauli Niinistö. It was previously speculated that the leader of the party and former presidential candidate [[Sari Essayah]] would run again.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.satakunnankansa.fi/paakirjoitukset/keskustelu-on-paikallaan/ |title=Keskustelu on paikallaan |website=Satakunnan Kansa |date=24 April 2016 |access-date=2 October 2016 |language=fi |archive-date=11 October 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161011100900/http://www.satakunnankansa.fi/paakirjoitukset/keskustelu-on-paikallaan/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, after Essayah announced that she would not seek the candidacy, the party convention decided to back Niinistö.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9786011 |title=Kristillisdemokraatit tukee presidentinvaaleissa Sauli Niinistöä |website=Yle |date=19 August 2017 |access-date=19 August 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

===Väyrynen's candidacy===
Former Center Party politician and three-time presidential candidate [[Paavo Väyrynen]] announced that he would run as an independent candidate if he managed to gather the 20,000 signatures required from his supporters in time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9725168 |title=Paavo Väyrynen neljättä kertaa presidenttiehdokkaana – jos löytyy riittävästi kannatusta |website=Yle |date=15 July 2017 |access-date=15 July 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> By 26 November, he had gathered around 15,000 signatures.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/presidentinvaalit2018/art-2000005465173.html?ref=rss |title=Paavo Väyrynen on saanut kokoon noin 15 000 kannattajakorttia – vielä runsas kaksi viikkoa aikaa |work=Ilta-Sanomat |date=26 November 2017 |access-date=26 November 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> On 5 December, he announced that he had gathered the needed 20,000 signatures.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005477082.html |title=Keskustan jäsen Paavo Väyrynen sai kasaan presidenttiehdokkuuteen vaadittavat 20 000 kannattajakorttia – Vanhanen: "Hän on kilpailevan puolueen ehdokas"|website=Helsingin Sanomat|date=5 December 2017 |access-date=5 December 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

==Campaign==
===Funding===
The parties budgeted about as much for their campaigns than during the last presidential election in 2012. Ahead of the election, the campaign teams budgeted as follows: Niinistö 1,000,000–1,500,000 euros, Haatainen 550,000 euros, Haavisto and Vanhanen 500,000 euros, Torvalds 400,000 euros, Kyllönen 250,000 euros and Huhtasaari 200,000 euros.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/taloussanomat/art-2000005425414.html |title=Uutissuomalainen: Haatainen lähtee presidentinvaaleihin isoimmalla budjetilla – Niinistö ei paljasta vielä |work=Taloussanomat |date=27 October 2017 |access-date=27 October 2017 |language=fi}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://demokraatti.fi/niinisto-pyrkii-jatkokaudelle-merkittavasti-vahemmalla-rahalla/ |title=Niinistö pyrkii jatkokaudelle merkittävästi vähemmällä rahalla |website=Demokraatti |date=28 October 2017 |access-date=28 October 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> Väyrynen didn't leave the notion ahead of the election, but revealed afterwards that his team had collected 162,000 euros for the campaign.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-10128633 |title=Paavo Väyrynen keräsi vaalirahaa yli 160 000 euroa, listassa ei mainita lahjoittajien nimiä |website=Yle |date=22 March 2018 |access-date=29 March 2018 |language=fi}}</ref>

After the election, Niinistö announced that the 300,000 euros that were reserved for the second round would be donated to the charity.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-10138193 |title=Sauli Niinistö jätti vaalirahoitusilmoituksensa – lahjoittaa ylijääneet rahat hyväntekeväisyyteen |website=Yle |date=28 March 2018 |access-date=29 March 2018 |language=fi}}</ref>

===Debates===
The first presidential debate was organised on 30 October 2017 by the Finnish Business and Policy Forum at [[Finlandia Hall]]. All confirmed candidates took part, excluding Kyllönen, who was on a business trip. The event marks the earliest moment that an incumbent president had taken part in debates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.is.fi/kotimaa/art-2000005428944.html |title=Presidenttiehdokkaat ensimmäisessä vaalitentissä |work=Ilta-Sanomat|date=30 October 2017 |access-date=30 October 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> The debates continued at the [[University of Helsinki]] on 13 November, with Vanhanen being absent after being hospitalised for [[heart arrhythmia]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yle.fi/uutiset/3-9928626 |title=Matti Vanhanen joutui sairaalaan sydämen rytmihäiriöiden vuoksi – perui osallistumisensa illan vaalitenttiin |website=Yle|date=13 November 2017 |access-date=13 November 2017 |language=fi}}</ref> As Väyrynen became an official candidate only in early December, he was not invited to the first three debates and thus the first debate, that gathered all candidates together, was organised on 13 December 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hs.fi/politiikka/art-2000005489031.html |title=Faktaa, tulkinnanvaraisuuksia ja harhaanjohtamista – HS seurasi illan kiivasta vaalitenttiä, täältä löydät mitä puhuttiin ja mikä piti paikkansa |work=Helsingin Sanoamt|date=13 December 2017 |access-date=13 December 2017 |language=fi}}</ref>

